    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d202">

                <pb facs="0214" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg210"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94119/426: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The First Secretary of Embassy in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Salisbury</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-07-01T17:00:00+08:00">July 1, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received July 2—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>407. Embassy’s 377, June 20, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="85" xml:id="d202-f1"/> Wang Keh
                        Min’s<anchor n="86" xml:id="d202-f2"/> peace appeal.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Some kind of peace plans are now under consideration here, one being
                        reportedly sponsored by Japanese in Peiping and possibly another by Wang Keh
                        Min. The Japanese plan allegedly contemplates a division of China into five
                        regimes: (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) the present Mongol border
                        districts, (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) provisional government, (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">c</hi>) reformed government, (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">d</hi>) a national government regime, and (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">e</hi>) a southwest regime. No mention <pb
                            facs="0215" n="211" xml:id="pg211"/> of the withdrawal of Japanese
                        forces is made. Further details are lacking.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The motives apparently are not [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">a</hi>) a genuine desire on the part of some local
                        Japanese as well as Chinese to see the end of hostilities, (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">b</hi>) a hope of creating disunity in the National
                        Government, and (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>) a maneuvering for personal
                        power. A number of reports that Generals Kita and Terauchi may go, in which
                        case Wang’s position would be weakened and political changes could be
                        anticipated, indicate that the third motive may be important.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>The proposal outlined above can scarcely be acceptable to the various
                        elements composing the National Government although it may prohibit [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">presage?</hi>] the future course of events if
                        hostilities and Japanese military successes continue.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>The question of amalgamation of the Peiping and Nanking regimes seems to
                        be indefinitely suspended. A new administrative plan is the proposed
                        creation of a special military affairs organ to be in control of all such
                        organs already existing in China.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Embassies, Hankow, Nanking, Tokyo, Consulate General Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Salisbury</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
